WEBVTT

n01
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.400
Every day, hospitals produce thousands of medical images.

n02
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:14.600
Each one holds information that could change someone's life.

n03
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.500
This is Noé.

n04
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:27.400
He works weekends at a university hospital, helping radiologists organize and review X-rays, CT scans, and MRI images.

n05
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.400
Noé: (to himself) How does anyone review all of this carefully?

n06
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:42.000
Noé: With so many images every day... how do you make sure nothing important is missed?

n07
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.800
Dr. Salizar: That's exactly why we're developing something new.

n08
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:57.800
RadiologyML is an artificial intelligence system designed to help doctors identify patterns in medical images.

n09
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:05.600
Instead of examining every image from scratch, it learns patterns from examples that experts have already reviewed.

n10
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.800
Note: RadiologyML is fictional. Its score is a model output, not a diagnosis.

n11
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.200
To a machine, an image begins as numbers.

n12
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:24.200
During training, a model learns patterns in those numbers that can help distinguish one type of image from another.

n13
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:33.600
The more useful the training examples and feedback, the better the model may become at recognizing relevant patterns.

n14
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.400
Machine learning models can make mistakes.

n15
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:46.200
Doctors review their predictions, identify errors, and provide expert feedback.

n16
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:54.400
After careful validation, those corrections can help researchers improve future versions of the model.

n17
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.800
One day, the system flags an unusual pattern.

n18
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:07.400
Dr. Salizar sends the image to a specialist, who confirms that the finding needs urgent attention.

n19
00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:14.600
The technology hasn't replaced the doctor. It has helped the medical team notice something important sooner.

n20
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.400
But this raises a bigger question.

n21
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.200
How can a computer learn from examples?

n22
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:34.400
How does it recognize patterns, make predictions, and improve its performance?

n23
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.600
Welcome to Machine Learning.
